    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old.jpg" alt="Sigismund I the Old" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I the Old</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kulmbach_Sigismund_I_the_Old.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hans von Kulmbach, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy grew out of the Monastic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, the territory of the State of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, which had been a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Polish Crown since the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteen-years-war-1454-1466">Thirteen Years' War (1454–1466)</a>. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> spread among the laity, dissent grew against the Catholic rule of the Teutonic Knights. Their Grand Master, Albert, of a cadet branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>, had lost a war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and lacked the military means to assert the order's authority. His bishop, Georg von Polenz of Pomesania and of Samland, had turned Lutheran in 1523, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> urged Albert at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> in 1522 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1524 to make the land a secular principality.</p><p>On 10 April 1525 Albert resigned his office, became a Protestant and, in the Prussian Homage in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a>, was granted the title Duke of Prussia by his uncle, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-i-the-old">Sigismund I of Poland</a>, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-krakow">Treaty of Kraków</a>. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> he declared his conversion; only Eric of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komtur">Komtur</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klaipeda">Memel</a>, opposed him. On 10 December 1525 the Prussian estates set up the Lutheran Church in Ducal Prussia with the Church Order. The duchy was home to German, Polish (mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masuria">Masuria</a>) and Lithuanian (mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> Minor) speakers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The nobility changed little in the passage from the monastic state to the duchy, but its control over the dependent peasantry grew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>'s free peasants, the Kölmer, held free estates under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-law">Culm law</a> with about a sixth of the arable land, much more than in other lands of the feudal era. Taxation by the nobility, the conflicts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and the abrupt secularisation fed peasant unrest, and a rebellion broke out in Sambia in 1525. Its relatively well-to-do leaders, among them a miller from Kaimen and an innkeeper from Schaaken, demanded a return to the older tax of two marks per hide, a measure of about forty acres.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> had Christianized the rural population only superficially and built few churches. Old Prussian and Prussian Lithuanian peasants kept pagan customs in places, honouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perkunas">Perkūnas</a>, Potrimpo and Pikullos; Bishop George banned pagan worship in 1524 and again in 1540. He ordered native languages used at baptisms in 1524, and the rural population of native descent was thoroughly Christianized only with the Reformation. Ducal Prussia became known as a land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> and sectarianism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg.jpg" alt="Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, Elector of Brandenburg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frans_Luycx_-_Frederick_William,_Elector_of_Brandenburg,_at_three-quarter-length.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Frans Luycx, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The duchy was born of weakness: Albert had lost a war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and could not defend the order's authority by arms. Under the arrangement with the Polish Crown he nevertheless kept his own army. When the Sambian peasants rose in 1525, saying they were rebelling against the harsh nobility and not the duke, Albert, back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, called them to a meeting in a field, surrounded them with loyal troops and had them arrested without incident; the leaders were executed, and there were no more large-scale rebellions.</p><p>The duchy was drawn into the wars of its neighbours in the 17th century. During the Polish–Swedish wars it was administered from 1635 by the Polish statesman Jerzy Ossoliński. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-x-gustav">Charles X Gustav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> invaded Ducal Prussia and made it a Swedish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> in January 1656. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick William, the Great Elector</a>, joined Sweden in the Treaty of Marienburg for the promise of Polish provinces, and the Treaty of Labiau made him full sovereign; he then changed sides for Poland's counteroffer. In 1662 he entered Königsberg with his troops to force the capital's allegiance.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ii-hector"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joachim-ii-hector.jpg" alt="Joachim II Hector" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ii-hector">Joachim II Hector</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joachim_II_of_Brandenburg_by_Lucas_Cranach_the_Younger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lucas Cranach the Younger, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Administratively little changed from the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>. Although formally a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Polish Crown, Albert kept self-government for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, his own army, the minting of his own currency, a provincial assembly (the Landtag) and substantial autonomy in foreign affairs. The Church Order of 1525 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> the state church, the first Protestant one, and provided for visitations of parishes, first carried out by Bishop George in 1538; officials toured the duchy enforcing Lutheran teaching and penalising pagans and dissidents.</p><p>When Albert died in 1568 his teenage son Albert Frederick inherited the duchy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ii-hector">Joachim II of Brandenburg</a> won co-enfeoffment for his line, granted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lublin">Lublin</a> on 19 July 1569. As Albert Frederick became increasingly feeble-minded, Margrave George Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> became regent in 1577. Agreements with Sigismund III in 1605 and the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> of 1611 confirmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>'s claim, in return guaranteeing Catholic worship. George William was invested with the duchy in 1623. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junkers">Junkers</a> opposed Hohenzollern rule from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, and most of society kept the tie with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> as a safeguard against absolutism.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg.jpg" alt="University of Königsberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">University of Königsberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siegel-Albertina-Koenigsber.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>As the first Protestant state, established in a deal partly brokered by Luther, Ducal Prussia anticipated the settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> of 1555. Albert's marriage on 1 March 1526 to Princess Dorothea, daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-denmark">Frederick I of Denmark</a>, tied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>, and he was aided by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-the-pious">George, Margrave of Brandenburg-Ansbach</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> banned Albert for his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-von-cronberg">Walter von Cronberg</a> went on claiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> without success.</p><p>In 1544 Albert founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-konigsberg">Albertina</a> University in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, the main school for the Lutheran pastors and theologians of Prussia. A privilege from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> in 1560 gave it the rights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> University, making it one of the leading universities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. Because services were held in native languages, Albert appointed exiled Protestant Lithuanian pastors such as Stanislovas Rapolionis and Abraomas Kulvietis as professors, and the Albertina became a centre of Lithuanian language and literature. The duchy's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> later laid the ground for the Kingdom of Prussia.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia.jpg" alt="Frederick I of Prussia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia">Frederick I of Prussia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frederick_I_of_Prussia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Friedrich Wilhelm Weidemann, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In return for Frederick William's renunciation of the Swedish alliance, King John II Casimir Vasa recognised his full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over the duchy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-wehlau">Treaty of Wehlau</a> of 19 September 1657 and the Treaty of Bromberg of 6 November 1657, confirmed in the 1660 Treaty of Oliva. After almost 200 years of Polish suzerainty, the territory passed under the full sovereignty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. The population, whatever its language, resisted, fearing Brandenburg absolutism. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>, led by Hieronymus Roth, rejected the treaties and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> to be within the Polish Crown, until Frederick William forced the city's allegiance in 1662. The Polish-French Treaty of Jaworów of 1675 planned a return to Polish suzerainty, but it was never carried out.</p><p>Full sovereignty allowed Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-i-of-prussia">Frederick III of Brandenburg</a> to become King in Prussia in 1701, and the duchy was elevated to a kingdom. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772 the former duchy, with Warmia, was reorganised as the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>. The kingdom and Brandenburg were joined de jure only after the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1806.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Duchy of Prussia was the secularised state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, made a Lutheran duchy under Albert in 1525 as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Polish Crown. Inherited by the Hohenzollern of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1618, it won full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1657 against the wishes of many of its people, and in 1701 it became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>.</em></p>

